        "Subsection 5.2.4 — Contested Resource Collateral, Sovereign Permanence Doctrine, and Chad's 2006 to 2008 Unwinding. Yet Drill Beta adds one explosive domestic complication that neither Seychelles nor Ecuador faced directly inside their bond prospectuses: pledging mineral concessions whose underlying legal awards sit contested by mining-district populations and former licensees. Under international public law—anchored in UN General Assembly Resolution 1803 (XVII) of 14 December 1962 on Permanent Sovereignty over Natural Resources alongside modern consultation jurisprudence drawn from ILO Convention No. 169 and Free, Prior and Informed Consent (FPIC) norms—subsoil concessions carry heightened political and legal vulnerability whenever resource-backed loans collateralise un-audited district claims inside an executive treasury account. Remember Chad's cautionary sequence on the Chad–Cameroon pipeline: Chad initially enacted Law No. 001/PR/1999 creating a Future Generations Fund and independent revenue oversight under World Bank covenants, yet on 11 January 2006 Chad's National Assembly passed Law No. 002/PR/2006 abolishing that savings reserve and folding security spending into priority sectors.",
