        "Practice Callout — Why External Custody Protects Both Debtor and Districts. Critics sometimes assume offshore or external escrow custody strips sovereignty from borrowing governments. In practice, verifiable independent trust custody lowers borrowing spreads, lowers insurance premiums via DFC and World Bank guarantees, unlocks investment-grade enhancement as in GPS Blue Financing DAC's Aa2 wrapper, and protects successor ministries against corrupt emergency raids on natural endowments—turning shaky short-term promises into durable multi-decade public capital.",
        "Subsection 5.2.5 — Anchoring Drill Beta's Diagnostic Position. Drill Beta's fictional coastal state combines every tense edge of sovereign debt statecraft: post-conflict sovereign arrears, a conservation bond buyback, rich yet legally clouded mineral concessions contested by districts and ousted licensees, and an imminent national election falling squarely inside the restructuring horizon. Ministers and the London or New York creditor steering committee talk candidly (Interpersonal 3.4); coastal municipalities broadly favour marine reserve protection (Communal 3.2); and long-run ecological protection retains genuine baseline public sympathy (Intergenerational 3.1). Yet Institutional-Relational sits bruised at 2.4 in the Fragile band because no neutral custodian exists that bondholders, miners, former licensees, and trust monitors all trust to handle concession revenues cleanly. How the operator stages escrow release, insulates the election shock, clears the 55 per cent Succession Trap, and reads Custodial Inversion at closing constitutes the strategic test of Drill Beta.",
