        "Subsection 5.2.10 — Deconstructing the Sovereign Credit-Enhancement Stack in Debt-for-Nature Mechanics. To understand why Seychelles' 2018 Blue Bond and Ecuador's 2023 Galápagos Marine Bond achieved substantially lower interest coupons than distressed sovereign yield curves, analyse how external multilateral guarantees alter default-loss allocation before any cash enters local spending. In an ordinary distressed restructuring, external bondholders price severe fiscal expropriation risk straight into exit yields, demanding double-digit coupons that wipe out fiscal headroom within five years. Introducing a layered guarantee structure—comparable to IBRD principal wraps, GEF concessional interest subsidies, US DFC political-risk insurance, and IDB partial liquidity buffers—substitutes multilateral institutional credit standing for vulnerable domestic cabinet discretion across the senior tranche of cash flows. Inside Desk 07, that external guarantee architecture acts as the real-world institutional foundation holding Tranche 3 (Operational Commissioning) and Tranche 4 (Final Cession) resilient whenever domestic party coalitions fracture.",
